{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '65,124p' docs/design/datom.world.md; sed -n '405,438p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; sed -n '621,640p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; sed -n '786,829p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; sed -n '830,889p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '65,124p' docs/design/datom.world.md; sed -n '405,438p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; sed -n '621,640p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; sed -n '786,829p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; sed -n '830,889p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md\"","aggregated_output":"See [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\nsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\nexhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n\n**The system ABI.** `dao.stream` is the system's binary interface — the only\ncontract that crosses a trust domain. Nodes with no shared code (different\nruntimes, languages, or machines) interoperate purely through canonical stream\nvalues (`dao.jing` encodings, content-hash identities) and the\nappend/park/resume discipline. Interfaces below this line are shims, not peers:\na guest that cannot express a stream operation (a WebAssembly module's only\noutgoing capability is an imported function over linear memory) is served by a\nhost-local ABI — for the wasm lane, `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`\n([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) extension §E) — that terminates at the\nembedding composition, which alone translates service requests into stream\noperations. Two consequences are doctrine. First, what crosses a trust domain\nis a strict subset of what exists inside a process: closures and continuations\ncross only as portable plain data; host types, resources, and code artifacts\nnever do — the custody gates mark exactly this seam\n([`yin.vm.linker.md`](./yin.vm.linker.md)). Second, every new guest substrate\nspecifies its boundary as such a shim to the composition, never as a second\nsystem ABI, and a process-local ABI's definitions have exactly one home in the\nspecification tree. Naming doctrine: \"the system ABI\" is reserved for\n`dao.stream`; a guest-host interface such as `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` is always\nqualified (the guest-host service ABI, the wasm service ABI), never bare \"the\nABI\" in a definitional position.\n\n**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\nhappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\nappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\nidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\nno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\ncorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n\n**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\na timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\nnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\nConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\nto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n\n**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\nadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\ntimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\nwhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\nthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\nto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\ndeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\nmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\nnot an upward callback.\n\nHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\nplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\ntransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\nadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\nevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\nfor facts it owns.\n### E.1 The machine-state-as-data boundary and synchronization protocol\n\nA guest machine's execution state must materialize as plain host-readable\ndata at every service boundary; the host never reaches into guest-private\nstorage. Each task owns one imported, non-shared wasm32 linear-memory arena\nas a private execution cache; separate tasks receive separate arenas even\nwhen compiled Module objects are cached. A supplied `coremem` can implement\nthe same interface only with task isolation and allocation ownership; shared\nmutable guest state is not introduced.\n\nThe public CLJS VM remains a persistent record with ordinary map keys, not a\ntoken that requires callers to use `read-state`. A native embedder exposes\nthe equivalent immutable named-field state and explicit snapshot replacement;\nit need not implement Clojure records, but must preserve their transition\nsemantics. The `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI (§E.3) serializes these snapshots\nand checked value references. Treat linear memory as a private execution\ncache. The guest-agnostic synchronization protocol across every host/guest\nseam is:\n\n1. **Public entry / host-to-guest:** `step`, `run`, and restore accept the supplied state map as authoritative. Import the guest's declared machine-state fields (for the register machine's eight-field instance — `:pc`, `:registers`, `:frames`, `:continuation`, `:store-of`, `:value`, `:halted?`, and `:blocked?` — see [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.1), plus the current code layout and engine state. W1 imports unconditionally. A later cache may skip only after proving all relevant keys agree with its last materialized snapshot; a retained private token alone is not proof. In particular, externally `assoc`-modified state (such as `(assoc parked :pc 2 :halted? false)`) must execute the requested state. Import a copied or privately owned active state shadow so running an old or externally modified map never mutates another returned map.\n2. **Guest-to-host:** before every state-consuming `engine/*` call, including `handle-effect`, pinning, collection, scheduler, park/resume and module work, materialize all of the guest's declared execution fields onto the VM record. Materialize them also at every public `step`, `run`, restore or inspection return. Keep the record's protocol implementations so `module/gc-roots` can inspect the current map. Dense memory synchronization without map synchronization is insufficient.\n3. **Host completion:** the returned host state is the new authority, including its execution fields and every extension key. Apply only the current operation's specified post-service changes to that returned state, then reimport it into memory. Never overlay a pre-service header onto it. In particular, `handle-effect` sets `:value` even for nonblocking effects; preserve that value, resource/heap/GC updates, stores, counters, queues and custody fields. A plain non-tail primitive call, by contrast, only updates the guest's destination/program counter in the kernel and does not automatically set `:value`.\n4. **Seam site coordinate:** before a host service, the map contains the instruction's absolute site coordinate (`:pc`), unless the source transition explicitly advances it first (such as parking in the FFI path). Capture the boundary payload at the site; apply the operation's coordinate update exactly once after service completion. Mailbox state does not count as a completed guest instruction.\n\nThis synchronization barrier is also a GC safety requirement: `:cell/new` may\ncollect *inside* `handle-effect`, before the adapter receives its result. At\nthat moment map-based execution fields (`:value` and the guest's active\nregisters/frames/continuation) must already be current. Reuse the engine's\nexact logical heap tracing rules; arena retention of a host value cannot\nsubstitute for marking the heap cells that value references.\n\nThe program store is logically an immutable map, usually name-keyed but **not\nrestricted to names**: `:store-get`/`:store-put` accept `:data` keys,\nincluding keywords and numbers in the corpus. Its linear-memory\n### E.3 Service protocol: `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`\n\n*(Owner amendment note, 2026-10-19: By explicit owner ruling, the service\nABI is named `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`, renamed from `yin.register.host.v1` in\nr5 §4.1.1, and its canonical tables live in this section. Other\nspecifications reference these tables rather than duplicating them.)*\n\nThe following names define the **engine host implementation ABI**\n(`yin.vm.engine.host.v1`, r5 §4.1.1), not repository functions or a guest\ninstruction-set schema change. This protocol is a process-local shim, not a\nsecond system ABI: it terminates at the embedding composition, which alone\ntranslates service requests into `dao.stream` operations — `dao.stream` remains\nthe only contract that crosses a trust domain (`datom.world.md`, Streams → Host\nBoundaries → The system ABI). All runtimes supply identical imports. The\nshared runtime module imports `memory` (one wasm32, non-shared memory),\n`functions` (one original function table), and\n**`exchange(control_ptr: i32) -> i32`** from module name\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1`. Generated component modules import the same\nmemory/table and the service entry as needed, plus declared immutable `i32`\nbase-offset words. No import/export passes a JS value, `externref`, Rust\nwith exact magnitude. W0 tests `value→wire→value→wire` identity, including\ninteger `1` versus Float64 `1.0`, `±0.0`, infinities and multiple NaN\npayloads.\n\n#### E.3.4 Message envelope, task-local references, and required operations\n\nMessages are maps with ABI version, task id, sequence, acknowledgement,\noperation, input state revision, execution snapshot, argument\nvalues/references and newly defined arena values; responses carry sequence,\noutput revision, execution status, result, post-service state and newly\ndefined values or diagnostic. Every execution snapshot includes the guest's\ndeclared machine-state fields (for register machines:\n`pc`/`registers`/`frames`/`K`/`store-of`/`value`/`halted`/`blocked`; see\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.1), plus layout\ngeneration. Host-owned fields and immutable image/value data may be\nreferenced by previously acknowledged definitions; missing or foreign\ndefinitions refuse. Mutation is represented by a new state revision, not\nmutation of a saved snapshot. Unknown extension fields in named state are\npreserved. W0 emits golden request/response bytes and locks\nrequired-field/type checks for each operation; both sides use those fixtures,\nnot serializer defaults.\n\nArena-reference tag `13` is an internal task-scoped reference, not a portable\nvalue or a way for guest data to mint authority. Trusted capabilities\n(primitive, Closure, Continuation, typed effect, stream service) are\ninstalled by the composition or checked kernel constructors and recorded in\nthe trusted arena. Generic literal decoding cannot create them. Plain-data\ndescriptor maps in the service protocol may name a trusted constructor\noperation, but guest literal maps and FFI data never enter that operation\nchannel. The native value service implements the same owner/type/arity and\neffect-authorization checks as the reference. External continuation export\nresolves arena refs to plain payload values or refuses; it never exposes tag\n`13` as a guest transport representation. Park and resume records cross the\nhost boundary as plain data under the §3 entry key contract above.\n\n**Required operations:** handshake/capability-profile negotiation; value\ndefinition/lookup and trusted closure/continuation minting;\nfree-name/store/gensym services; primitive application with typed-effect\nauthorization and exact call-effect completion\n([`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §3.4); stream/effect/FFI transitions;\n`scheduler-round` and ready/parked restore; collection/pinning; admitted\nmodule attachment/spawn and closure/resource lift/lower. Each operation\nspecifies its reference CLJC transition and read/write field set in W0.\nRequests carry the materialized state before any service that can collect.\nPure guest body control, moves and guest closure calls stay in the guest; the\nservice is not allowed to “run the remaining program” to manufacture the\nexpected trace.\n\n### E.4 The embedder seam, single-engine-port rule, and deterministic traversal\n\n#### E.4.1 Host responsibilities, embedder targets, and `IVM` `step`/`run` contract\n\nThe existing CLJS engine is one conforming embedder of `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`;\na native service implementation shared by Wasmtime and Wasmer adapters is\nanother. Neither JS nor a browser is required to execute an emitted guest\nbinary. A Rust native embedder implements the guest-host service ABI and semantic services\nwithout invoking Node, embedding a JS engine, or delegating transitions to a\nCLJS subprocess. Wasmtime is required beside Node from W1; Wasmer joins the\nrelease gate by W5. Browser embedding uses the same ABI. JVM and Dart remain\nsemantic reference lanes; binding wasm into their application APIs is\nseparate from the required standalone native embedders. WASI stays outside\nthe VM core and its imports; a WASI-based embedder may implement stream\ntransports/FFI below the same ABI.\n\nRead authorities (source symbols are semantic specifications for every\nembedder; calls to existing CLJC helpers mean direct reuse in CLJS and\nequivalently tested native transitions, not a requirement for native hosts to\nload Clojure): `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc`, `values.cljc`, `yin/vm.cljc`,\n`linker.cljc`, `semantic.cljc`, `semantic/derive.cljc`,\n`debruijn/register.cljc`, `debruijn_register_code.cljc`,\n`debruijn_register_effects.cljc`; the engine, semantic, linker and register\ndesign documents; and the semantic and register test corpora. In particular:\n- **A is the code identity**; R is the register image checksum. Do not publish wasm binaries as a new code identity, fetch code by a wasm checksum, or bypass admission.\n- Current `vm/machine-data?`, used by restore and payload validation, recursively accepts kernel-minted Closure/Continuation wrappers by their payloads, as well as exact-integer carriers. It is not identical to `plain-data?`. Arbitrary host functions/errors remain invalid. Preserve the predicate, not an approximate JSON test.\n- The current module-slice encoder in `engine/encoder` explicitly refuses reified and parked continuations with `:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:non-canonicalizable`. Preserve admissible native payloads and qualified refusals elsewhere; do not silently promise a new universal migration mechanism.\n\nThe host-facing adapter provides `create/load`, `attach`, `run`, `step`,\n`resume-parked(id, value)` and `restore-ready(base, entry, value)` over guest\ninstances. All entry/exit paths obey §E.1: callers can directly read or\n`assoc` the VM map keys, host calls see current map roots, and every\npost-host state is imported before guest execution continues. `read-state` is\na convenience, not a prerequisite for correct map contents.\n\nEach embedder supplies the same step/restore and scheduler contract over the\nABI. The CLJS adapter delegates to the shared engine; native adapters use the\nRust-kernel design's same `yin-kernel`/`yin-engine` services through the\nhandle/ABI facade, with no independent semantic service port. For the `step`\nmethod of `yin.vm/IVM`, run one guest instruction or the existing\n`scheduler-round` when inactive. For the `run` method of `yin.vm/IVM`, honor\n`engine/run-loop` ordering: active work first, then wait polling and ready\nqueue, then halt; wrap bridge dispatch with the equivalent of\n`ffi/maybe-run`. An optimized run chunk may execute many pure instructions,\nbut returns before every host service and exposes the same boundary state. It\nmust not poll early, reorder ready entries, or stop on a halt while ready\nwork remains. Preserve `halted-with-empty-queue?` rather than equating any\nprivate `HALTED` status with public completion.\n\nThe service mailbox is implementation glue, with no application callback. All\nadapters invoke primitives and sanctioned stream-handle operations through\nthe message contract. JS event handlers or native I/O completion handlers\nonly classify and append plain-data events; a separately driven pump polls\nthem and enters the adapter. External host effects remain request/response\nstreams. Neither a browser callback nor a native future resumes a retained\nwasm activation. Import execution completes synchronously with a response or\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
