{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'outcome|full|blocked|privileged|cursor|park|defprotocol|append' docs/design/dao.stream.md | head -60; sed -n '63,88p' docs/design/datom.world.md; sed -n '250,276p' docs/design/yang.wasm.md\"","aggregated_output":"14:1. **Everything is a Stream** — all IO and data flow through append-only\n45:5. A **cursor** is immutable interpreter-owned observation state for one\n49:identity. A handle is not portable identity. A cursor is not handle-owned\n50:state. A cursor is a value and has no identity of its own: a VM that lets\n51:programs advance a cursor keeps it in a mutable **cursor cell**, a box that\n52:has identity and is named by a sealed **cursor-ref** (`yin.vm`; the four-word\n53:vocabulary cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref and cell is defined in\n56:Seven operations range over them, each returning an outcome map (see\n61:| `create!`    | creation specification | handle outcome     |\n62:| `attach!`    | portable descriptor    | handle outcome     |\n63:| `descriptor` | handle                 | descriptor outcome |\n64:| `cursor`     | handle, anchor         | cursor outcome     |\n65:| `next`       | handle, cursor         | read outcome       |\n66:| `append!`    | handle, value          | write outcome      |\n67:| `close!`     | handle                 | close outcome      |\n76:- Reading is non-destructive. A reader advances its own cursor, not the stream.\n77:- Multiple cursors may observe the same retained value independently.\n78:- Interpreters own cursors and decide when to call `next`.\n82:- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap\n83:  outcome as data; the reader decides what missing data means.\n84:- All expected operational outcomes are data, not exceptions or callbacks.\n104:- Each operation's set of outcomes is **exhaustive**: a transport may produce\n106:  its declared nature (see Surfaces). An outcome is a map whose\n107:  `:dao.stream/outcome` key names the outcome with a qualified keyword. Every\n109:  `cursor`, whose success map carries the projected value.\n110:- `:dao.stream/outcome` is present in every result map and is never repeated\n111:  in the per-outcome tables below; the keys those tables name are required in\n114:  outcome, and a consumer must ignore keys it does not understand. This is\n117:- Each outcome set is exhaustive **for this version of the contract**. A\n118:  consumer that receives an outcome it does not recognize treats it as: not\n122:  outcome is a newer contract, not a defect in this host's assembly. A\n123:  transport may carry `:dao.stream/retry? true` on any non-`ok` outcome to\n125:- `:dao.stream/refused` is an outcome `cursor`, `next` and `append!` may\n127:  was observed and nothing was appended; not retryable unless the map\n133:  operation returns data: closed, full, blocked, end, gap, not found,\n134:  malformed input, and transport defects are all outcomes, never exceptions\n143:**Blocking (synchronous) IO.** The operation parks until it has an answer,\n147:requires a host that can park at all.\n160:true now, including the answer \"nothing yet, ask again.\" Nothing parks and\n165:has a defined data answer, and `:dao.stream/blocked` is `EAGAIN` — this\n166:position holds nothing yet. `append!`'s `:dao.stream/full` is the same\n167:answer for writes. No operation in this contract parks, and none takes a\n172:cannot yet know its answer returns the outcome true at the moment it is\n190:operations, the same outcome sets, the same control flow — which is what\n207:`:dao.stream/blocked` retries `next` when it chooses; retry cadence is the\n212:example a wake token list on `append!`), which the open-map rule already\n232:  no failure outcome.\n253:value it cannot encode is reported as data like any other outcome.)\n310:  contract does not answer and does not reserve room for — an outcome or key\n332:`create!` outcomes:\n341:`attach!` outcomes:\n366:a caveat rather than a new outcome. `attach!` defers a **remote confirmation**:\n370:outcome would have to return no handle — leaving the caller an identity it\n412:honor — **reader** (`cursor`, `next`), **writer** (`append!`), **closable**\n420:is no operation for this contract to give an outcome. What a host does when\n422:answer such a call with a DaoStream outcome map — an outcome would imply the\n426:answering a question this contract does not ask, such as how far a cursor\n445:lacks, passes cursors through unchanged, and may present values under its\n450:one positioned, append-only, retained sequence that cursors can observe and\n454:— where the reading model applies in full. The log lives beside such a\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\nsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\nexhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n\n**The system ABI.** `dao.stream` is the system's binary interface — the only\ncontract that crosses a trust domain. Nodes with no shared code (different\nruntimes, languages, or machines) interoperate purely through canonical stream\nvalues (`dao.jing` encodings, content-hash identities) and the\nappend/park/resume discipline. Interfaces below this line are shims, not peers:\na guest that cannot express a stream operation (a WebAssembly module's only\noutgoing capability is an imported function over linear memory) is served by a\nhost-local ABI — for the wasm lane, `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`\n([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) extension §E) — that terminates at the\nembedding composition, which alone translates service requests into stream\noperations. Two consequences are doctrine. First, what crosses a trust domain\nis a strict subset of what exists inside a process: closures and continuations\ncross only as portable plain data; host types, resources, and code artifacts\nnever do — the custody gates mark exactly this seam\n([`yin.vm.linker.md`](./yin.vm.linker.md)). Second, every new guest substrate\n\nEmission is byte-deterministic: equal admitted register image, emitter/ABI version, and feature\nprofile yield byte-identical WAT and assembled `.wasm` binaries across runs and platforms.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Code generation strategy and all 23 opcode lowerings\n\n### 3.1 Choice among the alternatives\n\n| Strategy | Benefits | Costs / semantic obligations |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fully structured body functions with direct guest `call` | Small fast code for ordinary functions; conventional optimizer input | Captured continuations require every caller activation to be materialized and abandoned; arbitrary resume PCs need secondary entries; suspension cannot retain native stack frames. A shadow stack alone does not unwind native callers. |\n| One interpreter loop over instruction records | Small wasm binary, trivial append and pc resume | Runtime operand decoding and memory-indexed registers on every step; does not exploit known register operands as locals. Uses the same K0/W0 oracle; retained as a W6 optimization axis with services moved into wasm if serialized crossing cost dominates. |\n| **Compiled body dispatch plus trampoline** | Static register locals and operands, arbitrary entry PCs, appendable bodies, constant native stack depth, explicit continuations | More generated code than interpreter; indirect dispatch and spills at transfers; per-body branch tables may be large. |\n\nChoose the third. A body function has conceptual signature `(state-ptr i32, entry-index i32,\nbudget i32) -> status i32`; updated budget/state lives in the header. All bodies share the same\nsignature for a function table. The trampoline resolves absolute pc, calls one body function,\nthen loops on transfer. A body **never invokes a guest callee directly**. Guest call saves a\nreturn frame (unless tail), installs callee state, and returns TRANSFER. Return installs caller\nstate and returns TRANSFER; final return returns HALTED. The trampoline's wasm `call_indirect`\nis an implementation dispatch, not the guest call stack. On HOST_SERVICE the body has returned;\na shared runtime service thunk serializes the mailbox and invokes the `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`\nimport ABI (§4; [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3). It decodes the completed response\nor returns the blocked/error status. No guest activation remains live across an asynchronous\nwait. The same thunk and body binaries run on each embedder.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"**A separate guest-host ABI is justified for the current split implementation. Its indispensable reason is that wasm and the embedder jointly implement one machine whose internal values and state are richer than its portable stream protocol.** It is not justified merely because wasm uses imported functions. Nor does that reason make the current generic `exchange` protocol indispensable.\n\nI would keep the distinction between machine-internal services and stream communication, but simplify the former where possible. I would not turn register access, variable resolution or GC synchronization into stream history.\n\nThe premise “wasm cannot express a stream operation” is misleading. Wasm can express one through an imported function implementing that operation. That is no less a stream operation than calling a `dao.stream` handle from Rust. Indeed, the doctrine explicitly permits function-shaped stream-handle operations. The concrete stream contract returns immediate outcomes; `next` can return blocked and `append!` can return full. **Parking belongs to the interpreter/scheduler, not to the stream operation itself.** An `append/read/park` import surface would already combine two layers. See [dao.stream’s operation contract](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md).\n\nConsequently, synchronous execution alone is not an argument against stream imports. A synchronous `append!` returning an exhaustive outcome fits the model perfectly. The difficult question is what belongs on that interface.\n\n**A variable lookup is not an external effect.** Under the present implementation, `resolve-var` needs access to host-owned environments, stores, primitives and modules. Making that lookup an appended request would require one of two choices:\n\n- Treat the request as real stream communication: give it identity, scheduling, retained history, response handling and an explicit interpreter responsible for resolving it.\n- Immediately service the request through a designated host function and return its answer, while calling the interaction a stream.\n\nThe first is a substantial execution-model change. It creates observable events and potential suspension points where the guest instruction previously had none. The second retains the internal service interface under stream terminology.\n\nThe no-privileged-reader rule does not forbid request/response streams or an explicitly composed service interpreter. Other interpreters can observe the same requests. But it also does not supply a unique responder, authorize that responder to mutate this machine, or make every observer’s response interchangeable. Those responsibilities still need an explicit contract. A stream is not a substitute for specifying them.\n\nGC makes the separation particularly clear. Before a host allocation can trigger collection, the host must see the current roots. Publishing “here are my roots” into a stream does not establish that the collector has consumed them before allocating. You would need acknowledgement and ordering machinery—or retain the synchronous materialization barrier. That barrier is an implementation obligation between parts of one evaluator, not an effect for independently observing agents. Optional tracing can record it; correctness should not depend on making it public history.\n\nThe representation problem also survives a transport redesign. Canonical Jing CBOR serves portable values and their identities. Internal execution additionally needs task-local handles, trusted wrappers, precise numeric carriers, immutable snapshot ownership and values whose external transport must be refused. Renaming `exchange` to `append` does not make those values portable.\n\nThere are two honest choices:\n\n1. Keep the split machine and define a private representation binding.\n2. Move enough runtime functionality into wasm that only portable effects and stream operations cross out.\n\nUsing Jing CBOR wherever values are already canonical could reduce codec duplication. It cannot eliminate the distinction without restricting in-machine behavior or defining additional private representations. Stream operations themselves still need a wasm binding for handles, buffers and outcomes.\n\n**I would not defend one generic `exchange` as an architectural principle.** Its benefits are practical: one import signature, a common envelope, uniform validation and reusable response-buffer handling. Its costs are also real: another dispatcher, serialization on every service call, a large operation vocabulary and transport mechanics interwoven with execution.\n\nTyped imports can use only i32 arguments and results: checked handles, offsets, lengths and status codes. They do not require i64, externref or host objects in function signatures. A typed interface can still centralize encoding and buffer management. Conversely, “typed” imports passing arbitrary request maps would merely distribute the generic dispatcher across several names.\n\nFor the current host-owned Value arena, I would build a small, explicit runtime binding with two distinguishable categories:\n\n- **Internal machine operations:** value ownership, primitive execution, resolution, state materialization and other services needed to implement the guest instruction. These invoke the shared engine/kernel implementation.\n- **Stream-handle operations:** preserve `dao.stream`’s existing operation and outcome semantics, with the same custody and authorization checks. Blocking outcomes return control to the scheduler; no imported function retains a suspended guest stack.\n\nI would select generic versus typed calls by measured complexity and crossing cost, not by doctrine. Neither choice changes the system’s communication model.\n\nAn effect-only wasm runtime is the cleaner eventual boundary **if** it owns its values, resolution, frames, continuation machinery and collection. It can run until an effect, return explicit resumable state, and let the composition perform stream operations. But “no synchronous services” is not a thin interface change: it relocates the runtime. That is a valid competing architecture, subject to the same conformance obligations and shared-implementation constraint.\n\nMoving the interpreter entirely into the host is simpler again, but then the host is executing the register images. Unless wasm still performs meaningful execution work, that abandons the purpose of this lowering rather than improving its boundary.\n\nFinally, “process-local” must not be used as a synonym for “same trust domain.” If arbitrary independently supplied wasm is admitted, the import interface is a security boundary regardless of its name. The current justification depends on locally derived wasm being an implementation component of the evaluator, with checked capabilities and no authority to manufacture trusted values. Calling an interface a shim does not establish that condition.\n\nMy position is therefore: **retain a private runtime binding because the chosen implementation splits one machine across wasm and the host; let actual communication retain `dao.stream` semantics.** The separation is substantive. The generic mailbox, private TLV codec and broad service vocabulary are contingent engineering choices. I would challenge those choices on complexity and performance grounds, but I would not eliminate them by promoting machine-internal traffic into stream history."}}
